DAY 18 - PART A: THEORY
NodePort, LoadBalancer, and How kube-proxy Works
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. GETTING TRAFFIC FROM OUTSIDE
-------------------------------
ClusterIP is internal only. To reach an app from outside:

  NodePort       opens the same port (30000-32767) on EVERY node
                 client -> <any-node-ip>:31234 -> Service -> pod
  LoadBalancer   NodePort + an external load balancer in front
                 (cloud creates it; Week 7)
  Ingress        one entry point, HTTP routing by host/path (Day 19)

Each type builds on the previous:
  LoadBalancer  ->  includes a NodePort  ->  includes a ClusterIP


2. NodePort YAML
----------------
  spec:
    type: NodePort
    selector:
      app: web
    ports:
    - port: 80          # ClusterIP port
      targetPort: 8080  # container port
      nodePort: 30080   # optional; random if omitted

Downsides: odd port numbers, clients need node IPs, one Service per
port. Good for labs and some on-prem setups.


3. LoadBalancer
---------------
  spec:
    type: LoadBalancer

In a cloud, the cloud-controller-manager sees this and creates a real
load balancer (AWS NLB, Azure LB, GCP LB), then writes its address in
status.loadBalancer.ingress -> shown as EXTERNAL-IP.
In kind or bare metal, nothing creates it, so EXTERNAL-IP stays
<pending> unless you add something like MetalLB or cloud-provider-kind.


4. externalTrafficPolicy
------------------------
  Cluster (default)  a node can forward to pods on OTHER nodes
                     (extra hop, client IP replaced by SNAT)
  Local              only to pods on the receiving node
                     (keeps real client IP; nodes without pods fail
                     health checks so the LB skips them)


5. HOW kube-proxy WORKS
-----------------------
kube-proxy runs on every node (DaemonSet). It watches Services and
EndpointSlices and programs the kernel. Modes:

  iptables  (default in most clusters) chains of NAT rules
  ipvs      kernel load balancer, better with thousands of Services
  nftables  newer replacement for iptables (Kubernetes 1.31+)

iptables chain walk for a ClusterIP:
  KUBE-SERVICES             match destination ClusterIP:port
   -> KUBE-SVC-XXXX         pick an endpoint at random (probability)
       -> KUBE-SEP-AAAA     DNAT to 10.244.1.5:8080
       -> KUBE-SEP-BBBB     DNAT to 10.244.2.7:8080
NodePorts go through KUBE-NODEPORTS into the same KUBE-SVC chain.

Some CNIs (Cilium) can replace kube-proxy entirely with eBPF.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- NodePort = port 30000-32767 on every node.
- LoadBalancer = NodePort + external LB (needs a cloud or MetalLB).
- kube-proxy writes NAT rules; it is not in the data path itself.
